1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:05,061
In 1739, a Persian king rode into Delhi as
its master.

2
00:00:05,301 --> 00:00:07,074
When he left a few weeks later,

3
00:00:07,094 --> 00:00:10,601
he took the Peacock Throne and the
Kohinoor diamond with him.

4
00:00:10,841 --> 00:00:13,323
One historian says those days made it

5
00:00:13,343 --> 00:00:16,568
possible for a trading company to conquer
India.

6
00:00:16,808 --> 00:00:17,412
How?

7
00:00:18,332 --> 00:00:22,052
To answer that, meet four men: an emperor
who loved pleasure,

8
00:00:22,072 --> 00:00:25,423
a conqueror in a hurry, a governor caught
between them,

9
00:00:25,443 --> 00:00:28,366
and a minister who had already half walked
away.

10
00:00:28,787 --> 00:00:30,102
The emperor first.

11
00:00:30,122 --> 00:00:33,440
Muhammad Shah took the Mughal throne in
1719,

12
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:36,184
and he held it for twenty-nine years.

13
00:00:36,603 --> 00:00:41,235
People called him Rangeela, the colourful
one, for his love of music,

14
00:00:41,255 --> 00:00:43,190
poetry and a luxurious court.

15
00:00:43,611 --> 00:00:45,384
Only a few years before him,

16
00:00:45,404 --> 00:00:49,354
powerful nobles had been making and
breaking emperors at will.

17
00:00:49,374 --> 00:00:52,555
Muhammad Shah got free of them early in
his reign,

18
00:00:52,575 --> 00:00:55,821
but the throne never fully recovered its
authority.

19
00:00:56,241 --> 00:00:57,846
But while the music played,

20
00:00:57,866 --> 00:01:01,518
the empire's great governors were quietly
turning into kings.

21
00:01:01,938 --> 00:01:07,260
In 1724, Nizam-ul-Mulk, once the emperor's
own chief minister,

22
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:10,189
set up his own state in Hyderabad.

23
00:01:10,608 --> 00:01:13,767
Bengal, Awadh and Punjab drifted the same
way.

24
00:01:13,787 --> 00:01:19,020
They still bowed to Delhi, but they kept
their taxes and their armies close.

25
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:24,213
So by the 1730s the Mughal Empire still
looked enormous on a map.

26
00:01:24,233 --> 00:01:28,490
In reality, it was a throne resting on
borrowed shoulders.

27
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:32,352
Far from the court, the north-west
frontier,

28
00:01:32,372 --> 00:01:36,185
India's oldest gateway for invaders, was
left neglected.

29
00:01:36,604 --> 00:01:39,126
But the first warning came from the south.

30
00:01:39,547 --> 00:01:43,576
In 1737, the Maratha Peshwa, Baji Rao the
First,

31
00:01:43,596 --> 00:01:47,626
rode north and struck at the very edge of
Delhi.

32
00:01:48,045 --> 00:01:53,933
Rajiv Ahir's history says he reached the
capital with just five hundred horsemen.

33
00:01:53,953 --> 00:01:58,456
Other accounts describe a much larger
Maratha army behind him.

34
00:01:58,876 --> 00:02:04,103
Either way, the message was the same: the
emperor could not protect his own capital.

35
00:02:04,523 --> 00:02:08,275
Baji Rao rode home, but the lesson stayed
behind.

36
00:02:08,695 --> 00:02:13,302
Delhi had just been shown how weak it was.
Someone in Persia was watching.

37
00:02:14,672 --> 00:02:16,867
That someone was Nadir Shah.

38
00:02:17,287 --> 00:02:22,472
He was a soldier who had fought his way to
the crown of Persia in 1736.

39
00:02:22,492 --> 00:02:26,577
He was fast, he was ruthless, and his wars
needed money.

40
00:02:26,997 --> 00:02:30,151
India, rich but divided, looked like the
answer.

41
00:02:30,571 --> 00:02:37,377
In 1738 his army crossed the old border
into Mughal lands and took Kabul.

42
00:02:37,397 --> 00:02:39,902
Early in 1739, Lahore fell.

43
00:02:40,323 --> 00:02:43,511
In Delhi, the court argued about who
should lead,

44
00:02:43,531 --> 00:02:46,981
and the old Nizam's advice was heard but
not trusted.

45
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:52,089
Now Muhammad Shah had to do the one thing
he had avoided all his life.

46
00:02:52,109 --> 00:02:53,434
He had to go to war.

47
00:02:54,304 --> 00:02:57,907
In February 1739, the two sides met at
Karnal,

48
00:02:57,927 --> 00:03:02,082
about a hundred and twenty kilometres
north of Delhi.

49
00:03:02,503 --> 00:03:07,234
The Mughal camp was enormous, by one count
three hundred thousand people,

50
00:03:07,254 --> 00:03:09,707
including servants and camp followers.

51
00:03:10,127 --> 00:03:14,080
But Nadir's army was smaller, faster and
better drilled,

52
00:03:14,100 --> 00:03:18,266
with uniformed musketeers and light guns
carried on camels.

53
00:03:18,686 --> 00:03:23,982
Saadat Khan, the governor of Awadh, rode
out against the Persians and was captured.

54
00:03:24,402 --> 00:03:27,289
Within hours, the Mughal army was broken.

55
00:03:27,709 --> 00:03:30,573
Muhammad Shah was taken into Nadir's camp.

56
00:03:30,593 --> 00:03:34,692
The emperor of India had become a guest
who could not leave.

57
00:03:35,112 --> 00:03:38,162
The battle at Karnal was only the
beginning.

58
00:03:38,182 --> 00:03:40,814
The real damage happened inside Delhi.

59
00:03:42,185 --> 00:03:46,822
On the twentieth of March, Nadir Shah rode
into Delhi as its master.

60
00:03:47,242 --> 00:03:50,380
The next night, a rumour spread that he
had been killed,

61
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:53,426
and some of his soldiers were attacked in
the streets.

62
00:03:53,846 --> 00:03:56,405
On the morning of the twenty-second of
March,

63
00:03:56,425 --> 00:03:59,213
he ordered his troops to strike back at
the city.

64
00:03:59,633 --> 00:04:00,601
In a few hours,

65
00:04:00,621 --> 00:04:05,017
an estimated twenty to thirty thousand
people of Delhi were killed.

66
00:04:05,037 --> 00:04:07,389
The true number will never be known.

67
00:04:07,809 --> 00:04:10,728
Doors stayed shut. The markets fell
silent.

68
00:04:11,148 --> 00:04:15,167
Even so, the survivors were ordered to pay
an enormous fine.

69
00:04:16,037 --> 00:04:18,472
But the real prize was the treasury.

70
00:04:18,891 --> 00:04:24,104
Nadir's men emptied the imperial vaults
and the private safes of the nobles.

71
00:04:24,524 --> 00:04:29,700
By one estimate in Ahir's history, the
haul was worth seventy crore rupees.

72
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:31,293
A crore is ten million.

73
00:04:31,713 --> 00:04:37,093
He took the Peacock Throne of Shah Jahan,
the Kohinoor, and another great diamond,

74
00:04:37,113 --> 00:04:39,398
the Darya-i-Noor, the Sea of Light.

75
00:04:39,818 --> 00:04:44,052
The demands fell hardest on the nobles who
had promised him money.

76
00:04:44,072 --> 00:04:45,857
Saadat Khan was one of them.

77
00:04:45,877 --> 00:04:49,853
The book says that, under that pressure,
he took his own life.

78
00:04:50,273 --> 00:04:55,690
Persia also took every Mughal land west of
the river Indus, Kabul included.

79
00:04:56,110 --> 00:05:00,283
Before he left, Nadir placed the crown
back on Muhammad Shah's head.

80
00:05:00,303 --> 00:05:03,057
He left him on a throne without its
treasure.

81
00:05:03,477 --> 00:05:05,417
Nadir Shah went home in May.

82
00:05:05,437 --> 00:05:10,735
But he had shown everyone — Afghans,
Marathas, even a company of merchants —

83
00:05:10,755 --> 00:05:12,555
that Delhi could be taken.

84
00:05:13,925 --> 00:05:18,722
The historian Jon Wilson, in his book
India Conquered, puts it like this.

85
00:05:19,142 --> 00:05:24,568
“Nader Shah was Mughal emperor for only
fifty-seven days, in 1739,

86
00:05:24,588 --> 00:05:30,262
but those days created aftershocks that
transformed India's politics…

87
00:05:30,681 --> 00:05:34,637
Those fifty-seven days laid the ground
which allowed the East

88
00:05:34,657 --> 00:05:38,808
India Company to conquer territory in
India for the first time.”

89
00:05:39,229 --> 00:05:42,671
How could a Persian raid help a British
trading company?

90
00:05:43,091 --> 00:05:44,779
Because the money was gone.

91
00:05:44,799 --> 00:05:49,713
Delhi could no longer pay its armies or
reward the governors who stayed loyal.

92
00:05:50,133 --> 00:05:53,021
So the governors stopped waiting for
orders.

93
00:05:53,041 --> 00:05:56,325
Hyderabad, Bengal and Awadh ran their own
affairs,

94
00:05:56,345 --> 00:05:58,836
and the Marathas pushed further north.

95
00:05:59,256 --> 00:06:02,611
In a land of rival rulers, a company with
ships,

96
00:06:02,631 --> 00:06:07,956
guns and hired soldiers could pick a side,
and in time take land for itself.

97
00:06:08,376 --> 00:06:12,709
Not every historian gives fifty-seven days
that much weight.

98
00:06:12,729 --> 00:06:16,917
Many point to a decline that began long
before Nadir Shah.

99
00:06:17,787 --> 00:06:21,258
But here is what 1739 really shows.

100
00:06:21,677 --> 00:06:24,697
An empire's greatest treasure is not its
gold.

101
00:06:24,717 --> 00:06:27,538
It is the belief that it cannot be
touched.

102
00:06:27,958 --> 00:06:31,077
Nadir Shah carried off a throne and a
diamond.

103
00:06:31,097 --> 00:06:33,534
What he really took was that belief.

104
00:06:33,554 --> 00:06:36,809
And once it was gone, every rival moved at
once.

105
00:06:37,229 --> 00:06:41,396
So, was Nadir Shah's invasion the real
beginning of British India?

106
00:06:41,416 --> 00:06:43,870
Tell me what you think in the comments.

107
00:06:44,289 --> 00:06:46,950
Eight years later, Nadir Shah was dead.

108
00:06:46,970 --> 00:06:50,868
One of his commanders, an Afghan named
Ahmad Shah Abdali,

109
00:06:50,888 --> 00:06:55,611
would come back again and again — until a
battlefield called Panipat.

